// DEBUGGING THE API, THE GAME AND THE POC, in VS Code — and starting the whole system.
//
// THREE THINGS MAKE UP THE SYSTEM, and only one of them is a program you debug:
//   * the DATABASE — DynamoDB Local in Docker, on 127.0.0.1:8000 (`docker compose -f api/docker-compose.yml up -d`);
//   * the API — `api/src/dev-server.ts` on 127.0.0.1:8090, either over the in-memory twin or over that container;
//   * the GAME — Vite on http://localhost:8089/, with `VITE_API_BASE` pointing at the API.
// `tasks.json` holds them as tasks (`system: up` is the one-action start); this file holds the debugger routes.
//
// THE POC IS OUTSIDE THAT SYSTEM, AND THAT IS WHY IT HAS NO COMPOUND. It is the authoring app — the sequencer panel,
// the call editor and the browse view — and it talks to no API and needs no Docker: its ONE prerequisite is a built
// engine, because `poc/vite.config.ts` aliases `dancing-squared-engine` to `engine/dist`. So `poc: dev server` carries
// its own `preLaunchTask: "engine: build"`, and A COMPOUND MUST NOT PAIR IT WITH ANOTHER ENGINE-BUILDING MEMBER: two
// builds racing on `dist` is AGENTS.md trap 32, which is why every compound below carries its build at the COMPOUND
// level and on none of its members.
//
// THE COMPOUND AT THE BOTTOM IS THE F5 ROUTE (`system: game + api + db`). It runs `system: prepare` ONCE — the engine build,
// the container and the table — and then starts the API and the game as debug sessions. That is why neither member carries
// its own `preLaunchTask`: a compound's members start together, so two identical pre-launch tasks would be two engine builds
// writing `engine/dist` at the same time, which is the corrupt-dist failure AGENTS.md trap 32 describes.
//
// WHY THERE IS NO BUILD STEP FOR THE SOURCES. `node src/dev-server.ts` runs the TypeScript SOURCE (Node's type stripping), so
// a breakpoint lands in the file it is set in and there is no map to go stale. `dancing-squared-game` also resolves to
// source (`"import": "./src/puzzle/index.ts"`), so a breakpoint inside `game/src/puzzle/generator.ts` is hit while you
// are debugging the API — which is where a slow Daily's time is actually spent. The ENGINE is the exception: it resolves
// through the `vite.engine.ts` alias to `engine/dist`, which is why `engine: build` is part of `system: prepare`.
//
// `cwd` IS THE API WORKSPACE, because that is what `npm run dev -w dancing-squared-api` does. Nothing in the server
// depends on it — the corpus is located from `vite.corpus.ts` by `import.meta.url` and the config comes from `DSQ_*` —
// but a launch that differs from the real one is a difference waiting to surprise somebody.
//
// `console: "integratedTerminal"` IS LOAD-BEARING: the boot banner ("listening on …", "auth: dev", "seeded the local
// fixture …", "published …") only reaches a real terminal, and those four lines are usually the first thing you read.
{
  "version": "0.2.0",
  "configurations": [
    {
      // THE ONE YOU WANT MOST OF THE TIME. `DSQ_AUTH=dev` is what makes `x-dev-player: <name>` an identity — in any
      // other mode it is refused, which is why a probe or a `curl` with that header 401s unless this is set.
      "type": "node",
      "request": "launch",
      "name": "api: dev server",
      "program": "${workspaceFolder}/api/src/dev-server.ts",
      "cwd": "${workspaceFolder}/api",
      // `DSQ_HOST=0.0.0.0` PUTS THE API ON THE NETWORK, which is what a phone needs: the app and the API are two
      // listeners, and a LAN run that exposed only Vite would load the page and fail every request. The API prints a
      // REACHABLE FROM THE NETWORK line when it binds wider; in `dev` mode that also means anyone on the network can name
      // any player, which is why the login-mock route below is the one to use for a LAN run with accounts.
      "env": { "DSQ_AUTH": "dev", "DSQ_PORT": "8090", "DSQ_HOST": "0.0.0.0" },
      "console": "integratedTerminal",
      "skipFiles": ["<node_internals>/**"]
    },
    {
      // STOPS ON THE FIRST LINE. Use this when the process dies during boot: the dev server publishes today's
      // challenges BEFORE it listens, so a boot-time failure is one you cannot reach from a request.
      "type": "node",
      "request": "launch",
      "name": "api: dev server (stop on entry)",
      "program": "${workspaceFolder}/api/src/dev-server.ts",
      "cwd": "${workspaceFolder}/api",
      "env": { "DSQ_AUTH": "dev", "DSQ_PORT": "8090", "DSQ_HOST": "0.0.0.0" },
      "console": "integratedTerminal",
      "stopOnEntry": true,
      "skipFiles": ["<node_internals>/**"]
    },
    {
      // A SINGLE PROBE OR TOOL: open `api/test/*-probe.mjs` and press F5. The handlers are pure over three ports
      // (`ItemStore`, `Clock`, `IdentityOf`), so a probe gives you the REAL handler over a memory store and a fixed
      // clock — no socket, no seeding, and its checks tell you where you are. This is usually faster than the server.
      "type": "node",
      "request": "launch",
      "name": "api: current file",
      "program": "${file}",
      "cwd": "${workspaceFolder}/api",
      "console": "integratedTerminal",
      "skipFiles": ["<node_internals>/**"]
    },
    {
      // THE BOUNDARY JOB, which is where a challenge is actually GENERATED (a read never generates — it answers 202 and
      // defers). Steps outside the request path entirely, and it is the tool that costs the 9.4-15.1 s. `--day` and
      // `--levels` are optional and default to today and the served levels.
      "type": "node",
      "request": "launch",
      "name": "api: publish today's daily",
      "program": "${workspaceFolder}/api/tools/publish-daily.mjs",
      "cwd": "${workspaceFolder}/api",
      "console": "integratedTerminal",
      "skipFiles": ["<node_internals>/**"]
    },
    {
      // FOR A SERVER YOU ALREADY STARTED, e.g.
      //   $env:NODE_OPTIONS='--inspect=9229'; npm run dev:api
      "type": "node",
      "request": "attach",
      "name": "api: attach to 9229",
      "port": 9229,
      "restart": true,
      "skipFiles": ["<node_internals>/**"]
    },
    {
      // THE LOGIN MOCK. `DSQ_AUTH=emulate` serves the five `/auth/*` routes against local accounts and advertises them on
      // `/health`, which is the switch that makes the app draw the sign-in form at all: under the default `dev` mode there is
      // no account to sign in to, so `net/standing.ts` deliberately offers none — that is why the login UI looked untestable.
      // It signs and verifies REAL JWTs with the same verifier the deployment uses, so this is a mock PROVIDER, not a fake
      // session (AGENTS.md §6). Signup confirms immediately; add `DSQ_AUTH_CONFIRM: "1"` to exercise the code-entry step.
      "type": "node",
      "request": "launch",
      "name": "api: dev server (emulator)",
      "program": "${workspaceFolder}/api/src/dev-server.ts",
      "cwd": "${workspaceFolder}/api",
      // `DSQ_HOST=0.0.0.0` so the login mock is the LAN's too: this is the run to use from a phone, because a token
      // identity cannot be forged by whoever else shares the network (in `dev` mode it can).
      "env": { "DSQ_AUTH": "emulate", "DSQ_PORT": "8090", "DSQ_HOST": "0.0.0.0" },
      "console": "integratedTerminal",
      "skipFiles": ["<node_internals>/**"]
    },
    {
      // THE API OVER THE REAL STORE. Same program and same source as `api: dev server`, and the three `DSQ_STORE` variables
      // are the whole difference — `api/tools/dev-ddb.mjs` sets exactly these two, and this repeats them so a breakpoint
      // here is the same run as `npm run dev:api:ddb`.
      //
      // THE FIRST BOOT AGAINST A FRESH CONTAINER TAKES ~85 s: before it listens it seeds three levels and publishes today's
      // challenges, and every one of those writes is a real DynamoDB call. That is also the run that found the store's
      // `undefined` defect (`api/src/store/plain-item.ts` says what it was) — so if this hangs, read its terminal rather
      // than assuming the port is wrong.
      "type": "node",
      "request": "launch",
      "name": "api: dev server (dynamodb)",
      "program": "${workspaceFolder}/api/src/dev-server.ts",
      "cwd": "${workspaceFolder}/api",
      "env": {
        "DSQ_AUTH": "dev",
        "DSQ_PORT": "8090",
        "DSQ_HOST": "0.0.0.0",
        "DSQ_STORE": "dynamodb",
        "DSQ_DDB_ENDPOINT": "http://127.0.0.1:8000"
      },
      "console": "integratedTerminal",
      "skipFiles": ["<node_internals>/**"]
    },
    {
      // THE GAME'S DEV SERVER, started as a debug session so a compound can include it. Nothing in the APP is debugged from
      // here — the app runs in the browser, and `game: chrome against 8089` (or the `serverReadyAction` below) is where its
      // breakpoints live; this session's own source is Vite's.
      //
      // `serverReadyAction` OPENS THE PAGE WHEN VITE IS READY, with the debugger attached to it: `debugWithChrome` reads the
      // URL out of the "Local:" line, so the browser is never pointed at a port nobody is listening on. That matters in a
      // compound, where the API and the game start at the same moment.
      "type": "node",
      "request": "launch",
      "name": "game: dev server",
      "runtimeExecutable": "npm",
      "runtimeArgs": ["run", "dev:game:lan"],
      "cwd": "${workspaceFolder}",
      // THE TWO ENVIRONMENT SHAPES THE APP KNOWS, and the difference is who mints the session:
      //   * `VITE_API_BASE` alone — the API's `/auth/*` routes (the local emulator) or no sign-in at all (`dev`);
      //   * `VITE_API_BASE` PLUS `VITE_COGNITO_REGION` and `VITE_COGNITO_CLIENT_ID` — the real Cognito user-pool API, with the
      //     API started in `DSQ_AUTH=cognito` so it verifies the pool's tokens. Both pool values or neither: a build with one is
      //     a build with no provider, deliberately.
      //
      // `VITE_API_BASE=/api` IS WHAT MAKES A PHONE WORK, and it is a PATH rather than an address on purpose: the dev
      // server proxies `/api` to the API on loopback (`game/vite.config.ts`), so the app and its server are ONE ORIGIN and
      // ONE PORT. A phone that can load the page can therefore always reach the API — where a second port is a second
      // thing its network has to allow, and a second origin needs CORS. REPORTED as the symptom that produced this: on a
      // real phone the app loaded and the SIGN-IN FORM WAS MISSING, because `/health` (on :8090) never answered and
      // `signInOffered` reads the routes it advertises.
      //
      // THE PROXY IS DEV-ONLY. A deployed build sets its own absolute base (`VITE_API_BASE` at build time) and reaches
      // the API through the gateway, so nothing here leaks into a release.
      "env": { "VITE_API_BASE": "/api" },
      "console": "integratedTerminal",
      "serverReadyAction": {
        "pattern": "Local:\\s+(https?://\\S+)",
        "uriFormat": "%s",
        "action": "debugWithChrome"
      },
      "skipFiles": ["<node_internals>/**"]
    },
    {
      // THE SECOND WINDOW ON THE SAME PAGE. `npm run dev:game` binds `localhost` (`::1`) and NOT 127.0.0.1, which is why the
      // URL is spelled that way — AGENTS.md trap 28 has the refused connection that produced the rule.
      //
      // `webRoot` is the GAME, not the workspace, because that is where the served sources are: a breakpoint in
      // `game/src/main.ts` resolves through it, and the workspace root would map every file one directory too high.
      "type": "pwa-chrome",
      "request": "launch",
      "name": "game: chrome against 8089",
      "url": "http://localhost:8089/",
      "webRoot": "${workspaceFolder}/game",
      "sourceMaps": true
    },
    {
      // THE AUTHORING APP, AND THE ONE TARGET WITH NOTHING TO START ALONGSIDE IT — see the header for why it has no
      // compound: no API, no Docker, and the engine build is its only prerequisite.
      //
      // `preLaunchTask` IS THE ENGINE BUILD because `poc/vite.config.ts` aliases the engine to `engine/dist` (that alias
      // is about ONE engine instance, not only resolution), so a stale `dist` here reads in the browser as a missing
      // export rather than as a build in flight — AGENTS.md trap 32. The task is the file's existing one, so there is
      // one build command and not a second spelling of it in `runtimeArgs`.
      //
      // `npm run dev` RATHER THAN A BARE `vite`, for the reason `game: dev server` records for its `cwd`: the launch has
      // to be the command the README documents, or the two are a difference waiting to surprise somebody.
      //
      // `serverReadyAction` OPENS THE PAGE WHEN VITE IS READY, and it is the only thing that will: `poc/vite.config.ts`
      // sets `open: false` and pins `port: 5173`. The pattern is Vite's own `Local:` line, so the browser is never
      // pointed at a port nobody is listening on.
      //
      // NO `poc: dev:lan` HERE, and the omission is deliberate rather than forgotten: the game has a `dev:lan` script
      // (`vite --host 0.0.0.0`) and `poc/package.json` does not, so exposing the POC on the LAN is a two-file change and
      // does not belong in a launch-config commit.
      "type": "node",
      "request": "launch",
      "name": "poc: dev server",
      "runtimeExecutable": "npm",
      "runtimeArgs": ["run", "dev"],
      "cwd": "${workspaceFolder}",
      "preLaunchTask": "engine: build",
      "console": "integratedTerminal",
      "serverReadyAction": {
        "pattern": "Local:\\s+(https?://\\S+)",
        "uriFormat": "%s",
        "action": "debugWithChrome"
      },
      "skipFiles": ["<node_internals>/**"]
    },
    {
      // THE POC'S OWN BROWSER, for a server started by hand (`npm run dev` in a terminal). `poc: dev server` above cannot
      // do this: it owns the browser it opens.
      //
      // A `pwa-chrome` session on the same page as the game's, for the same reason, and with the same rule: `webRoot` is
      // the POC, not the workspace, because the app is served from `poc/` — a breakpoint in
      // `poc/src/sequencer-ui.ts` resolves through it, and the workspace root would map every file one directory too
      // high. 5173 is `poc/vite.config.ts`'s own `server.port`.
      "type": "pwa-chrome",
      "request": "launch",
      "name": "poc: chrome against 5173",
      "url": "http://localhost:5173/",
      "webRoot": "${workspaceFolder}/poc",
      "sourceMaps": true
    }
  ],
  // THE F5 ROUTE. `preLaunchTask` on the COMPOUND — not on its members — is what makes this one build and one table: the
  // members start together, so per-member pre-launch tasks would race the engine build against itself.
  "compounds": [
    {
      "name": "system: game + api + db",
      "configurations": ["api: dev server (dynamodb)", "game: dev server"],
      "preLaunchTask": "system: prepare",
      "stopAll": true,
      "presentation": { "group": "system", "order": 1 }
    },
    {
      // THE GAME AND THE API ON THE IN-MEMORY TWIN: no Docker, no table, and a boot in a second or two. This is the pair to
      // reach for while working on the client — the store's own contract is what the DDB route is for.
      "name": "system: game + api (memory)",
      "configurations": ["api: dev server", "game: dev server"],
      "preLaunchTask": "engine: build",
      "stopAll": true,
      "presentation": { "group": "system", "order": 2 }
    },
    {
      // THE LOGIN MOCK, which is what makes the account UI testable at all: the emulator serves `/auth/*`, so the app draws
      // the sign-in form and a signup/signin/signout loop works against local accounts. No Docker.
      "name": "system: game + api (login mock)",
      "configurations": ["api: dev server (emulator)", "game: dev server"],
      "preLaunchTask": "engine: build",
      "stopAll": true,
      "presentation": { "group": "system", "order": 3 }
    }
  ]
}
